{"body":"The previous standup output included a dedicated \"Issues Closed\" section (`is:issue is:closed assignee:\u003clogin\u003e closed:\u003eyesterday`). That section has been removed entirely and is not replaced — the new \"Commented on\" section only surfaces items the viewer *commented* on, not issues they closed without commenting. If preserving the old \"Issues Closed\" behavior is intended, it should be added back as another section; otherwise this should be called out in the PR description as a deliberate behavior removal.","line":788,"path":"src/githubService.ts"}
{"body":"The `is:pr is:open author:${viewerLogin}` query has no date filter, so it returns up to 30 of the viewer's open PRs regardless of when they were last touched. As a result, the \"Open PRs\" standup section will include long-stale open PRs that have had no activity in the last 24 hours, which is inconsistent with the rest of the standup (merged: last 24h; commented: last 24h). Consider adding `updated:\u003e${yesterday}` (or similar) so the section reflects recent activity.\n","line":786,"path":"src/githubService.ts"}
{"body":"The `commenter:${viewerLogin} updated:\u003e${yesterday}` query returns any item the viewer has ever commented on whose `updated` timestamp is within the last day — including items where the most recent activity was someone else's update (a new comment, a label change, an assignment, etc.) and the viewer's own comment is weeks/months old. The PR description claims this section shows items \"where the viewer posted a comment in the last 24 h\", but the GitHub Search API has no `commented:` date qualifier, so `updated:\u003e` is a loose proxy that can include unrelated noise. Consider documenting this caveat or post-filtering via the issue's comments timeline.","line":787,"path":"src/githubService.ts"}
{"body":"The collapsible repo header is a `\u003cbutton\u003e` that toggles a region's visibility, but it lacks `aria-expanded` (and the items region lacks an `id`/`aria-controls` association). Screen-reader users will not be informed whether the group is open or closed. Adding `aria-expanded={open}` to the header button would address this.","line":58,"path":"webview-ui/App.tsx"}
{"body":"The item button's icon stroke colors are hard-coded (`#89b4fa` for PRs, `#a6e3a1` for issues) via inline `stroke` attributes, which means the existing `.light-theme.liquid-glass` overrides cannot retheme them — they will keep their dark-theme palette colors on light mode. Other newly added su-* classes have light-theme overrides; for consistency these icons should use `currentColor` (or a CSS class) so they can be themed.","line":79,"path":"webview-ui/App.tsx"}
{"body":"All repo groups default to `useState(true)` (open), but there is no mechanism to collapse-all / expand-all and the open state is local to the component instance — so any re-render that unmounts/remounts the group (e.g. switching tabs in the modal between \"view\" and \"markdown\") will reset the collapse state. Consider lifting the open state to `StandupView` keyed by `section.key + repo` so it persists across view toggles.","line":42,"path":"webview-ui/App.tsx"}
{"body":"The error path posts `data: { date: '', sections: [] }`, which the webview will treat as a successful empty standup and display the \"No activity found in the last 24 hours.\" placeholder. The actual error message is hidden inside `markdown` and only visible if the user manually switches to the Markdown tab. Consider surfacing the error in the structured view (e.g. an `error` field on `StandupData`, or setting `date` to `today` and adding an explicit error section) so users see what went wrong.\n","line":494,"path":"src/SprintHubPanel.ts"}

{"body":"`getSavedMergeMethod` reads from `localStorage` unguarded. In some webview / privacy contexts `localStorage.getItem` can throw (e.g., `SecurityError`), which would crash rendering of the entire detail panel. Consider wrapping the read in a try/catch and falling back to the default (`'squash'`) if storage is unavailable or throws.\n","line":290,"path":"webview-ui/components/DetailPanel.tsx"}
{"body":"`handleMerge` writes to `localStorage` without any error handling. `localStorage.setItem` can throw (quota exceeded, storage disabled), which would prevent `setOpen(false)` / `onMerge(method)` from running and leave the UI in a bad state. Suggest try/catch around the write and still proceeding with the merge action even if persisting the preference fails.","line":329,"path":"webview-ui/components/DetailPanel.tsx"}
{"body":"A new CSS class `merge-dropdown-item-preferred` is added to the dropdown items, but it doesn't appear to be defined anywhere in `webview-ui/styles.css` (and isn't referenced elsewhere). Either add the corresponding style or drop the extra class to avoid dead/unused styling hooks.\n","line":349,"path":"webview-ui/components/DetailPanel.tsx"}

{"body":"Draft pull requests are not excluded from card quality flags. The PR notes/test plan say draft PRs are unaffected, but this only checks `item.type`, so a draft with >500 lines or no reviewers will still render these chips.
","line":147,"path":"webview-ui/components/ItemCard.tsx"}
{"body":"This can show “No reviewers” for PRs that have a team review request. The `reviewRequests` data model is populated only from `requestedReviewer` values that are `User`s, so team requests are filtered out before they reach `BoardItem`; using an empty array here makes the new card chip inaccurate for those PRs.
","line":155,"path":"webview-ui/components/ItemCard.tsx"}
{"body":"This introduces a second source of truth for the Large PR threshold; the detail panel still hard-codes the same value in its quality flag calculation. Keeping these thresholds separate makes it easy for card and detail flags to drift, especially when the threshold becomes configurable.
","line":150,"path":"webview-ui/components/ItemCard.tsx"}
